# platform.linux.fs

mm.fs.native and mm.fs.local on Linux: a directory of the host's file system
as an mm.fs volume, through the C++ standard library alone --
std::filesystem, with its std::error_code overloads, for the tree, and
std::filebuf for the bytes. mm.fs.local mounts the device map's directory.0,
or the working directory, writable, when the map names none.

The volume keeps mm.fs's Volume rules that Linux itself would not: a file
open for writing cannot be opened again, removed, or renamed, and rename
never replaces a target. contain_symlinks treats a link whose target lies
outside the root as absent. sync hands the bytes to the kernel; the standard
library has no fsync, so it does not promise they survive a power cut.

Four volumes may be attached at once, each with 32 open files and 8 open
directories.
